          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Payment service uses adapters and circuit breakers, with saga compensation.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 description: 'Payment and Notification services reach external providers through adapters protected by circuit breakers'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Web and mobile client accessibility",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The gateway serves both client types.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 description: 'single entry point for web and mobile clients'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Timely bike availability and location tracking",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "Location tracking scales well, but immediate removal from availability is not guaranteed (see A2/S7, unclear).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternative_evaluation.json A2/S7 unclear",
            "stage2_assumptions_gaps.json S2-G7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Rental lifecycle and time-based charging",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "The lifecycle is coordinated by a saga, but its compensations cannot be defined until the timing of payment relative to registration is decided (S2-G15).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 limitations: 'Saga compensation design depends on the undecided timing of payment relative to registration (S1-G17, S1-G22).'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Physical bike unlock by code",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "Code registration exists in the Rental service. Delivery of the unlock depends on the assumed lock interface (S2-G4).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 assumptions: 'Bike location and status reach the backend through an interface ... that can be wrapped by an adapter.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "SMS and email message delivery",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "A Notification service with adapters, driven by events.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 description: 'Notification services reach external providers through adapters protected by circuit breakers'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "User authentication and session management",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Identity service with Federated Identity; the gateway validates tokens. S2-G14 is open.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 selected_patterns: 'Federated Identity', 'API Gateway'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Usage history and administrative usage reporting",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "CQRS read models for history and hourly/daily aggregates.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternative_evaluation.json A2/S11 satisfied",
            "alternative_evaluation.json A2/S13 satisfied"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Ease of use for all riders",
          "status": "unclear",
          "rationale": "A2 does not claim to address this driver and has no element for it. Eventual consistency may harm the rider experience.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 addressed_drivers does not include 'Ease of use for all riders'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Service availability to riders",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Fault isolation between services keeps bike search working when Payment fails. This depends on S2-G6 (ability to operate the platform).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 rationale: 'a Payment provider outage does not stop bike search'",
            "stage2_assumptions_gaps.json S2-G6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Evolution of payment methods and jurisdictions",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Payment adapters inside an independently deployable Payment service.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 addressed_drivers: 'Evolution of payment methods and jurisdictions'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Low carbon footprint",
          "status": "not_satisfied",
          "rationale": "A2 states that it does not address this driver and that it has the highest infrastructure footprint.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A2 limitations: '... in tension with the driver \"Low carbon footprint\", which it does not address.'"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "alternative_id": "A3",
      "requirement_evaluations": [
        {
          "requirement_id": "S1",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Identity module handles registration, reached through the BFFs that 'act as the security boundary'. A3 describes edge protection and server-side token storage for browsers, but not encryption from the BFFs to the database or at rest, so the device-to-database clause is only partly addressed (S1-G1).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'The BFFs validate tokens from the Identity module (Federated Identity) and act as the security boundary'",
            "S1: 'end-to-end encryption of information between user device and app database'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S2",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Verification notifications are published as events through a transactional outbox and delivered by SMS/email adapters, so a code is never lost after registration commits. Broker propagation delay adds to delivery time, which matters only under the 30-second reading of S2 (S1-G4).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'side effects (verification and expiry notifications, ...) are published as events through a message broker using a transactional outbox'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S3",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The mobile BFF explicitly handles device-local biometric login; email/password goes through the Identity module. The flush rule relies on S2-G3.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'a mobile BFF (device-local biometric login, ...)'",
            "alternatives.json A3 assumptions: 'Biometric thumb-impression verification is performed on the device by its native authenticator'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S4",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Session rules live in the Identity module. The web BFF keeps browser tokens server-side, which lets that BFF track inactivity and end sessions there. Token/inactivity alignment for mobile is still open (S2-G14).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'a web BFF (...) that also keeps tokens server-side for browsers'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S5",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The mobile BFF provides a nearby-bikes view on top of the Fleet & Availability module. It relies on S2-G4 for location input.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'a mobile BFF (... code scanning, navigation, nearby bikes)'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S6",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The mobile BFF explicitly covers code scanning and navigation, and registration stays in a local transaction in the core. The 90-minute versus S10 conflict is requirement-level (S1-G40), and the unlock path relies on S2-G4.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'a mobile BFF (device-local biometric login, code scanning, navigation, nearby bikes)'",
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'Bike registration and removal from availability stay in one local transaction'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S7",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Registration and removal from availability happen in one local transaction, as in A1. Only side effects such as the administrator status refresh are asynchronous.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'Bike registration and removal from availability stay in one local transaction'",
            "alternatives.json A3 rationale: 'A3 keeps A1's strong consistency for S6/S7'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S8",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Payments module reaches the platforms through adapters with circuit breakers. Payment timing is open (S1-G17, S1-G22).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'External providers are reached through adapters with circuit breakers.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S9",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Expiry notifications are published as events through the outbox and delivered by the Notifications adapters. Overtime charges go through Payments.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'side effects (verification and expiry notifications, ...) are published as events'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S10",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Rentals module in the core owns duration and expiry, and nothing restricts the duration range.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'The same capability modules as A1'",
            "alternatives.json A3 addressed_drivers: 'Rental lifecycle and time-based charging'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S11",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Reporting module keeps CQRS read models for three-month history, and the web BFF supports downloads. Read models may lag (S2-G12). The description assigns history downloads to the web BFF only; mobile access to history is not described.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'A Reporting module keeps CQRS read models for three-month history'",
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'a web BFF (rider web access, history downloads, ...)'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S12",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The web BFF provides the administrator fleet status view. The status refresh arrives by event and may lag slightly; no freshness target exists (S1-G13).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'administrator fleet status and usage views'",
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'administrator status refresh' published as events"
          ]
        },
        {
          "requirement_id": "S13",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Under the S1-G34 interpretation, CQRS read models hold hourly/daily aggregated usage for administrators, which also allows aggregated rather than per-biker data (S1-G35).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'CQRS read models for three-month history and hourly/daily aggregated usage'",
            "alternatives.json A3 rationale: 'can hold aggregated usage for administrators'"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "quality_attribute_evaluations": [
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Security",
          "impact": "mixed",
          "rationale": "Keeping browser tokens server-side and verifying biometrics on the device both help. However, edge security must be implemented the same way in two BFFs, and the broker adds a component (qualitative).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'keeps tokens server-side for browsers'",
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'edge security must be implemented consistently in both BFFs'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Privacy and Regulatory Compliance",
          "impact": "mixed",
          "rationale": "Aggregated read models can limit how much personal data administrators see, but read models replicate usage data, so retention rules must cover them too (S2-G17).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 rationale: 'can hold aggregated usage for administrators'",
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'Read models replicate usage data, so GDPR retention rules must also be applied to them.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Interoperability",
          "impact": "positive",
          "rationale": "Adapters with circuit breakers for all external providers, plus asynchronous delivery of notifications.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 selected_patterns: 'Adapter (Integration Gateway)', 'Circuit Breaker'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Portability (multi-client accessibility)",
          "impact": "positive",
          "rationale": "Separate mobile and web BFFs tailor the API to each client's capabilities (biometrics, scanning, navigation on mobile; downloads and administration on web). This rests on S2-G10.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'fronted by two Backends for Frontend: a mobile BFF ... and a web BFF'",
            "alternatives.json A3 rationale: 'using BFFs to tailor each client'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Usability",
          "impact": "positive",
          "rationale": "Client-tailored flows (nearby bikes, navigation and scanning in one mobile BFF), plus availability data that is never stale, support ease of use. This is a qualitative architectural contribution only.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 addressed_drivers: 'Ease of use for all riders'",
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'mobile BFF (device-local biometric login, code scanning, navigation, nearby bikes)'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Performance (timeliness of bike availability and location)",
          "impact": "mixed",
          "rationale": "Availability changes are immediate, and reporting load moves to read models, removing one source of contention seen in A1. Location updates still go to the single core database, and the administrator status refresh is asynchronous (qualitative).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'Bike registration and removal from availability stay in one local transaction'",
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'A Reporting module keeps CQRS read models'",
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'The core still scales and fails as one unit'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Availability",
          "impact": "mixed",
          "rationale": "Notifications and reporting are decoupled through events and circuit breakers, but the core still fails as one unit, and the broker and BFFs are extra components that can fail.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'The core still scales and fails as one unit, although notifications and reporting are decoupled through events.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Modifiability",
          "impact": "positive",
          "rationale": "Modules and adapters as in A1, and new event consumers can be added without changing the core flows. The outbox and idempotent consumers add complexity.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 rationale: 'events to decouple notifications and reporting from rider flows'",
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'Reliable event publication (transactional outbox) and idempotent consumers add implementation complexity.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "quality_attribute": "Sustainability (resource efficiency)",
          "impact": "mixed",
          "rationale": "A single core keeps the footprint below A2's, but the two BFFs and the broker add components compared with A1 (qualitative; no metric, S1-G52).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'Two BFFs plus a broker add components compared with A1'"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "driver_evaluations": [
        {
          "driver": "Security of user, biometric and payment data",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "Device-local biometrics, server-side browser tokens, Federated Identity and payment adapters are in place. Device-to-database encryption is not described (see A3/S1), and edge security is duplicated across two BFFs.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternative_evaluation.json A3/S1 partially_satisfied",
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'edge security must be implemented consistently in both BFFs'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "GDPR and regional regulatory compliance",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "Aggregated read models help with data minimisation for administrators, but replicated read models enlarge the retention scope, and the jurisdiction, residency and retention questions are open (S1-G43, S2-G16, S2-G17).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'Read models replicate usage data'",
            "stage2_assumptions_gaps.json S2-G17"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Third-party payment platform integration",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "The Payments module uses adapters with circuit breakers.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'External providers are reached through adapters with circuit breakers.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Web and mobile client accessibility",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "There is a dedicated BFF for each client type. Mobile access to history downloads is not described explicitly (see A3/S11).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'a mobile BFF ... and a web BFF (rider web access, ...)'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Timely bike availability and location tracking",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "A transactional S7 makes the change immediate, and the nearby-bikes view sits in the mobile BFF. Location writes share the core database.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternative_evaluation.json A3/S7 satisfied",
            "alternative_evaluation.json A3/S5 satisfied"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Rental lifecycle and time-based charging",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Rentals and Payments run in one core, so no saga is needed; expiry notifications are delivered reliably through the outbox.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'side effects (verification and expiry notifications, ...) are published as events through a message broker using a transactional outbox'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Physical bike unlock by code",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "The mobile BFF handles code scanning and the core handles transactional registration, but delivery of the unlock to the bike relies on the assumed lock interface (S2-G4, S1-G16).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 assumptions: 'Bike location and status reach the backend through an interface (e.g. a lock or telematics provider) that can be wrapped by an adapter.'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "SMS and email message delivery",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Events published through the outbox, delivered by adapters with circuit breakers. Fallback behaviour is unspecified (S2-G13).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'transactional outbox'",
            "alternatives.json A3 selected_patterns: 'Adapter (Integration Gateway)', 'Circuit Breaker'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "User authentication and session management",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Identity tokens are validated by the BFFs, and the web BFF keeps sessions server-side. S2-G14 is open.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 description: 'The BFFs validate tokens from the Identity module (Federated Identity)'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Usage history and administrative usage reporting",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "CQRS read models for history and hourly/daily aggregates, kept separate from transactional load.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternative_evaluation.json A3/S11 satisfied",
            "alternative_evaluation.json A3/S13 satisfied"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Ease of use for all riders",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "A3 is the only alternative with an architectural element for this driver (client-tailored BFFs, including navigation). Ease of use still depends on client design, which no alternative describes.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 addressed_drivers: 'Ease of use for all riders'",
            "stage2_assumptions_gaps.json S2-G10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Service availability to riders",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "Side effects are decoupled and providers sit behind circuit breakers, but the core is a single point of shared failure. Acceptability depends on S1-G50.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'The core still scales and fails as one unit'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Evolution of payment methods and jurisdictions",
          "status": "satisfied",
          "rationale": "Modules and adapters as in A1.",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 addressed_drivers: 'Evolution of payment methods and jurisdictions'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "driver": "Low carbon footprint",
          "status": "partially_satisfied",
          "rationale": "A3 has one deployable core but adds two BFFs and a broker, so its footprint lies between A1 and A2 (qualitative; S1-G52).",
          "evidence": [
            "alternatives.json A3 limitations: 'Two BFFs plus a broker add components compared with A1'"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

